ACCEPT.md mirrors §3 row by row: expected → actual, with the evidence link. For
GUI: reference image and actual screenshot side by side, same viewport, plus the
behaviour checks (buttons hit real keypaths, fields take input —
`lesson.parity-was-correctness-not-decoration`). Send Kjetil the artifacts, not a
description of them. G3 is his word.

### Build ACCEPT.md from the evidence, and have it checked (HD-0077, 2026-10-04)

Kjetil reads ACCEPT.md and TESTRESULT.md as fact. In HD-0077 round 2 a first draft, written
from the session's memory while the evidence lay on a machine out of reach, had 27 errors
when an independent thread held it against the evidence files. What worked after that:

- **Numbers, times, commits and quotes are filled in by code.** The documents are templates
  with placeholders. A values module reads each evidence file and asserts the counts it
  expects, and the build refuses to write a document while a value is missing. What exists
  only in the session, with no evidence file, is named as such at the top of the document.
- **An independent thread checks the built documents before Kjetil gets them.** It gets the
  documents and read-only copies of the evidence, and a bounded brief: which sections, what
  to compare, what not to judge (language, whether the rules are good), one verdict per
  statement (`stemmer`, `FEIL`, `upresis`, `uten bevis`, `uten bevis (oppgitt)`,
  `kan ikke kontrolleres her`), a time limit, and a list of what it did not reach. The
  second check held 160 statements
  against the evidence: 132 matched, 1 was wrong, 5 were imprecise, 9 had no evidence file
  and 13 could not be checked from the package.
- **Corrections made after the check are unchecked.** Say so in the document.
- **The same holds for a status message, and for the README beside a script.** On 2026-10-06
  a message went to Kjetil a quarter of an hour before the documents it pointed to were
  checked. The check found eleven places: an inference written as a measurement («the tool
  would have run with umask 077», which follows from sudo's documented default and was not
  measured on the host), the reviewers' remarks rendered as «a fault» and «all corrected»,
  an answer left out where a safeguard was described, and a time for when a run would be
  done, with no basis given. Write at each statement whether it is measured, read or
  inferred, give an estimate as a span with what it rests on, and let the check come before
  the message. Report: `Deliverables/PDD_korrespondanse-fornyelse_2026-09-30/panel/FABEL_DOK_RUNDE4.md`.
- **Every question Kjetil is asked to try has been asked of the running service first.** A
  draft used «Hva er Arancini?» as its example. The service does not answer that question.
- **A statement about all tests is a statement about each of them.** The one error the check
  found was «røde først» claimed for every new or changed test. Twelve changes to existing
  tests had come in with the code and were never run red.
- **The check comes after the last number, and it reads the message too.** On 2026-10-06 a
  check read ACCEPT.md while 27 of 174 measured calls were still to be run again; the final
  numbers went in afterwards, unchecked. A second check right before sending, with the brief
  «recompute the numbers from the raw files, and hold the message to Kjetil against them»,
  found no wrong number and eight other things: the message put a weakness on a test run
  made before the commit, though the raw answers from the live site showed the same; it was
  milder than the document in four places; and one explanation in the document did not add
  up. The message is a document like the others.
- **Give the checker the commit, not your copy.** The same day a README went into a commit
  built on a stale copy from earlier in the session, and 104 lines from three commits fell
  out. The check before the commit did not find it: it was pointed at the same copy. Build
  every changed file on the git object of the parent commit (from a Cowork VM, where git
  must not be run against the mount: `haven-git-workflow`, «Reading a commit from the Cowork
  VM without running git»), and tell the checker to find the base itself.
- **Set a hard time limit, and make the checker watch the clock.** «Aim for about 15
  minutes» ran to 28–45 minutes three times. «TIDEN ER HARD: 12 minutter. Kjør `date -u` når
  du starter og underveis. Når tiden er gått, leverer du det du har, med resten under ‘Ikke
  kontrollert’» held four times out of four (9–13 minutes). Order the brief by importance,
  so what is cut is what matters least.
  On 2026-10-07 a brief with a soft limit («Svar på under ti minutter») and five questions in no
  order took 17 minutes for a short status message. Two briefs the same day that gave the limit
  and the questions to take first took 9 and 8 minutes; one of them also said what the check was
  not («Dette er en kontroll av én side, ikke en gjennomgang av spesifikasjonen. Prioriter
  spørsmål 1, 2 og 4.»). The times are read from the file times of brief and report. They were
  three different tasks, so this is weak evidence.
- **The checker is a model with a quota: have a second one ready, and name the model.** By the
  thread's own log and notes, a document check on 2026-10-06 at about 21:30Z was broken off: the
  subagent on model `fable` («Fabel») answered HTTP 429, weekly limit, until 2026-10-08 10:00Z.
  By the same notes, the updated documents were not written to the task folder until the check
  had run, the next day, with `model: "opus"` on the Agent call. Write in the report, in
  `STATUS.md` and in the message to Kjetil which model read the text, and that it was not the
  usual one.
- **A check holds up the thread that ordered it.** Tool calls in one block run one after
  another, and a call to a subagent holds everything behind it until it returns. Put the
  long queue job in first, then start the check, and say in the brief what that job changes
  in the working tree while the checker reads (on 2026-10-06 a red-run step set one line
  into a source file for two minutes; the brief said so and pointed to the commit).
- **Evidence names what it proves.** A chain step that requires «red first, then green»
  before a commit must require it for the exact files it commits: put the md5 of each file
  in the marker line and match on it. Otherwise a file corrected after the check is
  committed on the old evidence. Example:
  `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/mac/strakstiltak-avvik4-20261006.sh`.

The briefs, the build scripts and the templates as they were run:
`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/DOKUMENTBYGG-20261004.tar.gz`. The reports:
`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/uavhengige/KONTROLL-AKSEPTUTKAST-RAPPORT.md` and
`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/uavhengige/KONTROLL-AKSEPT-2-RAPPORT.md`. From
2026-10-06: `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/grunnlag-20261006/kontroll/` has the fixed
rules for a read-only checker (`REGLER-K1.md`), two short briefs with a hard limit
(`OPPGAVE-K9.md`, `OPPGAVE-K10.md`) and their reports (`K9-FERDIG.md`, `K10-AVVIK4.md`).

### A correction is new text: have it checked, and say which round nobody read (HD-0077, 2026-10-07)

Every check of a correction found errors in the correction: three rounds on one plan (two serious
findings, two serious, then none and three errors), and the same in code, where a fix let a
wait-listed guest take a table after the time had started. So:

- Plan the check of the corrections when the first check is planned, with the before and after
  folders and the diff ready for the checker.
- Stop when the remaining findings are small, and write in the document and in the message to Kjetil
  that the last round has not been read by an independent thread.
- A correction must not point to a file before the file is there. Let the script that writes the
  text require that the report or copy it refers to exists at that path
  (`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/grunnlag-20261007/kontroll/k3/rett_pdd4_2.py` does).

## P6 — Learn, so it is not repeated

For every deviation, bug, misunderstanding, or blocked test: add a lesson
(`purpose_dev.py lesson add <folder>` or edit the JSON) with symptom, documented
cause (write `ukjent` rather than guess), a prevention phrased so it can become a
`derivedTest`, the purposeRefs and tags that should surface it next time, and the
source path. Then:

- Promote `candidate` purposes that proved durable into a package or into Book 23
  via the capture process (Book 23 §8) — never mutate Book 23 canonically without
  Kjetil.
- If a lesson repeats a pattern, add the prevention as a `derivedTest` on the
  relevant package purpose so it is enforced, not just remembered.
- If the lesson changes how a *thread* should work (a tool, a route, a pitfall in
  a skill or prompt), also route it there: from the HAVEN root
  `python3 CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py capture --what … --kind pitfall --target skill:<name>`,
  edit the target, `applied <id>`, `sync --id <id>` (skill `haven-learning-capture`).
  The lessons register says what went wrong; the learning ledger says which skill
  changed because of it, and whether that change reached every client.
- Run `purpose_dev.py validate` and fix what it reports.

## Boundaries

- Purposes never grant rights; a match is not an authorization (Purpose-Bound
  External Action Contract). `purpose://prompt.unknown` fails closed.
- Do not document planned behaviour as implemented. Do not mark a gate approved on
  Kjetil's behalf. Do not replace an image with a description.
- The method is meant to become cells in HAVEN; this skill and the JSON files are
  the seed and the reference implementation, not the product.

## Asking for a gate

A gate request is a message Kjetil must be able to act on without searching. It carries, every time:

1. The **absolute path** of the document — `/Users/<user>/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN/CellProtocolDocuments/Deliverables/<folder>/<file>.md`, not a bare filename.
2. What he is deciding, in one or two sentences.
3. What is deliberately not done, named.

He stated the path rule 2026-09-09 and had to repeat it 2026-10-01 after a G2 request that named
only `PLAN-E8.md`. Repeating a rule costs him more than the rule saves.

He had to say it a third time on 2026-10-05: «Du må legge med hele banen til filer som
dette: ACCEPT.md». A hand-over message, the questions put to him with AskUserQuestion, and a
reference like «ACCEPT.md §8» had only the file name. The rule holds for **every mention in
every message**, not for the first. Check it mechanically before a message is sent:
`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/mac/sjekk-melding-20261005.py <file with the message text>`
flags every word that ends in a file extension and does not start at a root.

The checker sees file extensions only. Two kinds of mention pass it and still break the
rule: a file named by a description («akseptdokumentet», «README-filen for
PalazzoScaffold»), and files delivered as cards in the chat, which show only the base name.
Put the full path beside a description the first time it is used in a message, and list the
full path of every delivered file in the message that goes with it. On 2026-10-06 a G3
request passed the checker with both gaps; they were corrected in the next message.

**A message with several choices gets its own check before it is sent (HD-0077, 2026-10-07).**
The overview that put ten questions to Kjetil after a night's work went out with only the path
checker run on it; the documents behind it had been checked three times. An independent thread
that read the hand-over document half an hour later found four places where the message said too
much or too little, and he got them as corrections. P5 already says the check reads the message
too. What was missing was a brief for it. Give the checker the message, the documents and
`STATUS.md`, and ask:

- **Is the recommended alternative named for every choice?** One choice read «under every answer,
  or only under answers that name a dish», without saying which one the plan recommended.
- **What does a bare yes accept?** «If you only say approved, the recommended choices apply» makes
  every recommendation the message does not name, and every deviation it leaves out, part of his
  yes. List them. If a recommended alternative is narrower than a rule that is live (here: which
  answers carry the allergen line), say so in the message and keep the live rule until he has
  chosen.
- **Does every sentence about what was done when match `STATUS.md`?** «By then I had done what I
  could on the main task» stood against a log that showed the independent checks and three rounds
  of corrections still to come. It was the sentence his decision on a condition rested on.
- **Are summary words held to the standard of the documents?** «The kernel is read» was in the
  message; the plan named the reader and the files (P0).

Report: `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/grunnlag-20261007/kontroll/K9-OVERLEVERING.md`. The
corrections as sent: `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/grunnlag-20261007/RETTELSE-MORGENMELDING-20261007.md`.

**Old messages can arrive again.** In a Cowork session on 2026-10-06, two earlier messages
from Kjetil came up again as new ones, word for word, each time the answers to a question
card were read, and once as the opening text of a resumed turn (four times that day). One
was the path rule above, the other a change of direction from the day before. Before you
act on a message that corrects you or changes direction, hold it against his earlier words
in `STATUS.md` and §0. If it is the same text, treat it as the same message, tell him once
that it came again, and go on. On the night to 2026-10-07 the same message arrived three
times as a new turn while he slept; each got its answer, the second and third in short form
with the time of the first.

The correspondence thread saw two forms on 2026-10-07: an older message glued in front of a new
one with no space between them («…manuelle operasjoner.Jeg har godkjent prøveparet»), and the
same older message once more when the answers to two question cards were read. It wrote «Kjetil
sendte meldingen fra 06.10 kl. 10:10Z på nytt» in `STATUS.md` and «gjentok ordrett» in its
memory. It had seen the message arrive, not him send it. Write «the message arrived again», read
the new part as his message, and ask him once.

## A status message between gates (correspondence thread, 2026-10-07)

Kjetil, 2026-10-06, in a task whose purpose was encrypted correspondence with attachments out to
two people: «Dette sier meg ingenting om hva status er eller hva jeg skal gjøre. Formålet er å få
ut correnspondence til vegar og victoria, med støtte for vedleg og kryptert kommunikasjon. Hva
gjenstår for å få testet med dem. De har ikke fått noen ny installerer pakke. Og det er altfor
mye manuelle operasjoner.» By the thread's own note from that day, the status he had been given
was about a queue that stood still. Which messages he was answering is not kept word for word.

- **First: where the purpose stands for the people it is for.** The status line on the G1 page
  the day after read «Vegar og Victoria har ikke fått noe nytt, og ingen ny installerer er
  bygget.» When the answer is «nothing yet», that is the first sentence.
- **Then what he must do, numbered, and no more points than there are things to do.** Each is an
  act he can carry out from the message alone: the full path of the file, the address, the
  deadline.
- **Then what remains before the purpose is met,** as the links in order and who has each, not
  as a log of what was done. A figure of the links helps.
- **Work on a side track is called a side track,** in a line or two. Here an old channel had to
  be kept alive while the new one is built.
- **A decision is put as a question card (AskUserQuestion) with two or three plain
  alternatives, or as numbered questions.** By the thread's own note, a choice written into a
  long message on 2026-10-06 (where the owner's keys should lie) was not answered in his next
  message, which answered a later request sent the same evening. Put as a card the day after,
  with one other, both were answered. Five numbered points sent later that day got a reply
  number by number: two approvals, two questions back, and under the fifth number his direction
  on another matter on the page (by the thread's own note of the times, within the hour). These
  are single observations, not a comparison.
- **A question uses his words, and says what a thing does before asking about it.** Two of those
  five came back as questions: «Hva gjør assistentidentiteten?», and «Hva mener du med at vedlegg
  kan gå til main? Vi skal ikke lagre vedlegg i git så forklar hva du mener.» The first had named
  a thing, «en delegert assistentidentitet», without saying what it does. The second had said
  «vedlegg» and `main`; by the author's account it meant the code that handles attachments, and
  the main branch. The check before sending read an earlier draft with four questions and was
  asked six things; none was whether he could answer the questions without the repo's
  vocabulary. The page was changed after the check, and the changes were not checked again. Ask
  the checker that question, on the page as it will be sent.
- **A request with a deadline says what follows when the deadline passes, and names every later
  step that is his.** A draft asked him to approve two access requests within 24 hours. It left
  out that the invitation is then spent and a new one is needed, which by the checker's reading
  meant a new swap on the host and a new restart of staging. It also left out the step after:
  his approval of the two recipients' own requests, each within 24 hours. The check before
  sending found both:
  `Deliverables/PDD_korrespondanse-fornyelse_2026-09-30/panel/FABEL_BYTTEMELDING.md`.
- **No message when nothing has changed for him.**

## Completion checklist

- [ ] §0 verbatim intent, all claims audited with status
- [ ] Every leaf purpose has goal (outcome, signals, verifier) and a §5 test
- [ ] Lessons from `lookup` quoted in §4 and reflected in §2/§5
- [ ] §3 expectation contract filled; GUI rows point at approved `images/` files
- [ ] STATUS.md gates set only by Kjetil's word; `validate` is clean
- [ ] Every gate request to Kjetil names the **absolute path** of the document he is being asked to approve
- [ ] TESTRESULT.md has real output for every §5 row (or `blocked` + reason)
- [ ] ACCEPT.md pairs expected/actual for every §3 row
- [ ] ACCEPT.md and TESTRESULT.md are built from the evidence files and were checked by an
      independent thread; every question Kjetil is asked to try was measured on the running service
- [ ] The independent check came after the last number was filled in, and it read the message
      that goes to Kjetil; every file in that message has its full path, also files sent as cards
- [ ] A message that puts choices to Kjetil was read by an independent thread before it was sent: the
      recommended alternative is named for each choice, and what a bare yes would accept is listed
- [ ] A status message opens with where the purpose stands for the people it is for, then his
      numbered actions, each with its deadline and what follows when it passes
- [ ] Where «few steps» is a purpose, the handgrips are counted per party, the owner included,
      from the images
- [ ] Every question to Kjetil uses his words and says what a thing does before asking about it
- [ ] §2 has a measured row for every step before the one being changed
- [ ] New lessons recorded; candidate purposes promoted or dropped

## Independent purposes must have independent failures

When one run serves two purposes, a failure in the lesser one must not take the greater one
with it. Measured 2026-09-30 in HD-0077: the host deploy served two purposes — *get the
guest and waiter surfaces onto the web* and *let a model phrase the concierge's answers*.
The model key turned out to be unusable, the host script returned exit 4, and the deploy
script treated that as fatal. The surfaces were never deployed, because a **wording** feature
had failed.

The purposes were independent. The failure handling was not.

Checks to run whenever a script or a pipeline serves more than one purpose:

- **List the purposes the run serves**, and for each, name what must be true for it to be
  met. If purpose B can fail while purpose A is still met, B's failure is a warning.
- **Order the steps so the shared prerequisites come first**, then the independent parts, so
  a failure late in B cannot undo A.
- **Never infer that a purpose was met from an input being present.** The deploy assumed
  "a key file existed, therefore the model is on" and set the acceptance expectation from
  that — which would have failed the whole acceptance run on something that is not a defect.
  Measure the outcome on the host and set the expectation from the measurement.
- **Say which purpose failed, in the report**, so the next reader does not think the whole
  run is broken.

---
name: haven-git-workflow
description: Manage HAVEN Git, PRs, CI and coordinated package releases across repositories, preserving worktrees and linking verified source changes to Workbench release evidence.
---

# HAVEN Git Workflow

Use this skill for git operations in HAVEN workspaces. It is intentionally more
specific than generic git advice because these repos often have dirty worktrees,
submodules, companion documentation repos, and cross-repo runtime coupling.

## Core Rules

- Start with `git status --short` in every repo you may touch.
- Never revert user changes unless the user explicitly asks.
- Keep commits scoped to the actual task.
- Prefer small branch/commit names with the `codex/` prefix unless the user
  requested another convention.
- Treat `CellProtocol`, `CellProtocolDocuments`, `CellScaffold`, and `Binding`
  as separate repos even when they appear together in a workspace.
- When code changes protocol behavior, include documentation changes or explain
  why docs were not changed.
- Before pushing code that triggers paid CI, complete the local verification
  gate below. Reporting an omitted check does not make the candidate tested.
- Do not use destructive git commands without explicit user approval.

## Repo Map

Common local repos:

- `/Users/kjetil/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN/CellProtocol`
- `/Users/kjetil/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN/CellProtocolDocuments`
- `/Users/kjetil/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN/CellScaffold`
- `/Users/kjetil/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN/Binding`
- `/Users/kjetil/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN/DiMyDocuments`

## Required Workflow

1. Identify repos touched by the task.
2. Run `git status --short` in each touched repo.
3. Separate user-existing changes from your changes.
4. Inspect diffs before staging.
5. Stage only intended files.
6. Commit with a clear message when the user asks for a commit or when the
   workflow explicitly requires one.
7. Push/open PR only when requested or when the user has asked for publish/PR.
8. Include test results and any dirty unrelated work in the final handoff.

## Dirty Worktree Handling

- If unrelated files are dirty, leave them alone.
- If a file you must edit is already dirty, inspect it and work with the current
  contents.
- If user changes make the requested task ambiguous or unsafe, stop and ask.
- Do not "clean up" generated or untracked files unless the task is specifically
  cleanup.
- **Commit what you wrote, not what is in the tree.** Measured 2026-10-02: another thread's
  design package lay untracked in `PalazzoScaffold/Deliverables/` (26 files) when a deploy
  commit for the concierge was prepared. The commit script ran `git add -A` and would have
  swept it in. Stage with an explicit exclusion (`git add -A -- . ':(exclude)Deliverables'`)
  or by naming the files, print what is held out, and check `git diff --cached --name-only`
  before committing. A push guard that stops on "anything besides X" must name the untracked
  paths it tolerates, and why. Pattern:
  `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/mac/deploy-commit-20261002.sh`.

## Worktrees created from the Cowork VM

For a **new worktree created by this task with `--no-checkout`**, initialize its
index from `HEAD` before staging a sparse report. Verified 2026-09-29:
`sparse-checkout set` alone left that new worktree's index empty, so tracked
files appeared as staged deletions. In the newly created worktree only,
`git read-tree HEAD` followed by `git sparse-checkout reapply` populated the
index correctly while preserving the new report files. Inspect
`git diff --cached --name-status` before committing and require exactly the
intended paths. Do not use this index initialization on a user's existing dirty
checkout or index.

A worktree created with `git worktree add` from the Cowork VM writes the VM's own
path into `<worktree>/.git` and into `.git/worktrees/<name>/gitdir`:

    gitdir: /sessions/rcw-…/mnt/<repo>/.git/worktrees/<name>

That path does not exist on the Mac. Every git command run inside that directory
on the Mac fails with `fatal: not a git repository`, and Codex logs
`Resolved gitdir path … does not exist`. Measured 2026-09-16 and again 2026-09-28.

- **Never hand the user a git command that `cd`s into such a worktree.** Use the
  branch name from the main checkout instead:
  `git push origin <branch>`, `gh pr create --head <branch> --body-file <worktree>/PR_BODY.md`.
  The branch lives in the shared `.git`, so that works.
- Do not run `git worktree repair`: it makes the worktree valid on the Mac and
  invalid from the VM. Pick one side per worktree.
- Such worktrees are registered `locked initializing`. `git worktree remove
  --force` fails until the `locked` marker is moved aside, and removing the
  directory needs file deletion enabled for that repo
  (`device_request_delete_permission`). Order that works: move `locked` → `rm -rf`
  the worktree → `git worktree prune` → `git branch -D` → `git branch -dr` for the
  stale remote-tracking ref.
- Building in such a worktree still works. Two flags are required, and both are
  about the environment, not the code:
  - `--disable-sandbox` on the swift command. Without it SwiftPM dies on
    `sandbox-exec: sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted` / `Invalid manifest`,
    because SwiftPM sets up its own sandbox for `Package.swift` and that cannot
    happen inside Codex's sandbox.
  - `HAVEN_SWIFTPM_RUNNER=<HAVEN>/CellProtocol/Scripts/haven-swiftpm.sh` when using
    `scripts/run_swift_bounded.sh`, because the runner otherwise resolves
    `haven-swiftpm.sh` through `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, which fails here.

Note that CellProtocol's own `Scripts/haven-swiftpm.sh` has no disk capacity gate —
that gate lives only in CellScaffold's `run_swift_bounded.sh`.

### Reading a commit from the Cowork VM without running git (2026-10-06)

Git must not be run against the mounted folders from the VM. To see what a file was in a
given commit, read the objects directly:
`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/grunnlag-20261006/gitles.py` resolves a commit to its
tree and a path to its blob, from loose objects and from pack files, and verifies the sha1
of everything it returns. It only reads.

    import sys; sys.path.insert(0, "HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/maal/grunnlag-20261006")
    from gitles import Repo, blobsha_bytes
    r = Repo("PalazzoScaffold/.git")
    blob = r.blob_at("<full commit sha>", "README.md")   # blob id of the file in that commit
    typ, data = r.get(blob)                                # its bytes
    assert blobsha_bytes(open("my-copy/README.md", "rb").read()) == blob

It takes a full commit sha; read `.git/HEAD` and the ref file (or `packed-refs`) for a
branch. Run it with `python3 -B` so no `__pycache__` is left in the folder.

Use it before a changed file goes into a commit package, and in the brief for whoever checks
the package: the base is the file in the parent commit, not a copy from earlier in the
session. On 2026-10-06 a README built on a stale copy dropped 104 lines from three commits,
and the check before the commit was pointed at the same copy.

A scratch checkout in `/tmp` that has a `.git` directory is not necessarily a valid
repository after a half-finished clean-up. Test it with `git rev-parse --git-dir`, not with
`[ -d .git ]`, before a script reuses it. Seen 2026-10-06 with
`/tmp/cp-verify/CellProtocol`; the folders had to be moved aside and fetched again.

### Building a new branch in a worktree through the queue (HD-0077, 2026-10-07)

Work that must not touch the tree that mirrors what is deployed is built in its own worktree, driven
from the Cowork VM through the queue:

- One script on the Mac with the modes `tilstand`, `opprett`, `test`, `test:<filter>` and `commit`
  (`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-0077/mac/bordbestilling-wt-20261007.sh`). `test` copies the files named
  in a list from a staging folder into the worktree, prints their md5, builds and tests. `commit`
  commits exactly the files in a second list. Run `test` immediately before `commit`.
- The commit list may only name files that have a change. A file without a change stops the script
  before `git add`, and nothing is committed.
- `launchctl submit` restarts a job when it ends. A build started that way begins again in the
  seconds before the job is removed: read the numbers from the first run, and guard the script
  against a second.
- A branch named `losen/**` triggers no workflow in PalazzoScaffold
  (`PalazzoScaffold/.github/workflows/palazzo-image.yml` runs on `main`, `pdd/**` and `deploy/**`). The
  Linux build is untested until the branch is pushed under such a name, which also builds an image.
  Pushing is Kjetil's word.
- Never run git against the mounted folder, not even `git rev-parse`. To see what the main tree
  stands on, read `.git/HEAD` and the ref file as files, or use `gitles.py`.

## Pushing from the Claude cloud container

The cloud container (the `Bash` tool in a Cowork/cloud session, not `device_bash`)
has network access to GitHub, but its git proxy injects a credential only for
repositories that are added as **sources** of that session. For any other repo,
`git push` fails with `403 … not in this session's authorized repository set … add
the repository to the session's sources`. Measured 2026-09-26 against
`Digipomps/CellProtocolDocuments`.

- Ask the user to add the repository under the session's sources; then push and
  `gh pr create` work directly from the cloud.
- Otherwise ship the change as a patch and let the Losen queue push it on the Mac
  as a `skript` job: write `<name>.patch` (`git format-patch -1 --stdout`) and
  `<name>.pr.md` into `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/CPD-PUSH/` and queue
  `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/CPD-PUSH/push-patch.sh <name>` (README there has the job
  lines). It does `git fetch origin main` → `git worktree add -B losen/<name>
  HAVEN/_worktrees/CPD-<name> origin/main` → `git am --3way` → the repo's own checks →
  `git push -u origin --force-with-lease` → `gh pr create` with the commit subject as
  title. It never touches the main checkout (which may sit on someone else's branch)
  and is idempotent. Proven 2026-09-26 (forerunner, PR #25 in 9 s). Verify the
  patch's md5 on the Mac before queuing (see `_losen-queue/LES_MEG.md`, item 12).
- The agent job spool (`.agent-spool/inbox/`, run by `.agent-tools/agent_job_runner.py`
  under launchd) is a third route for the push, and only for the push. Measured
  2026-10-03 with a job in `HavenAgentD`: `git push --dry-run origin
  HEAD:refs/heads/<new-branch>` answered `[new branch]`, so the spool reaches GitHub
  with keys that can push. `gh` is not in that runner's search path
  (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'gh'`), so `gh pr create`, `gh pr view` and
  `gh api` fail there even though `gh` is on its allowlist. Open the PR through the
  Losen queue as a `skript` job (it has Homebrew in its path), or by hand. The spool
  refuses mutating git in the primary checkouts of CellScaffold, CellProtocol,
  Binding and CellProtocolDocuments; use a worktree. Put a measuring job with
  `gh api user --jq .login` first if a later step depends on `gh`.

## Local dependency overrides and provenance gates

Measured 2026-10-08 in the correspondence candidate: SwiftPM local package
overrides removed their remote entries from Package.resolved. A CellProtocol
source archive without .git then failed runtime build-metadata and skeleton
parity provenance gates, despite matching the published source commit. Use an
independent detached Git checkout at the verified public revision when a gate
requires Git provenance. Never synthesize HEAD or write an arbitrary hash to
make the gate pass. If runtime metadata reads a lock file, provide an explicit
metadata lock exported from the tested candidate base with only verified pins
changed; this does not prove the remote dependency graph resolves. Restore the
remote lock before committing, preserving unrelated pins. Pair a dependency
change with the parity matrix provenance revision only after comparing the
schema, configurations, and native renderer sources; retain fixture outcomes
and any untested consumer classification. Record local overrides separately
from release-tag/remote-resolution evidence.

## SwiftPM dependency fetching stalls

Observed 2026-09-29 with Apple Swift 6.4 on macOS 27: seven concurrent dependency
fetches stopped writing their partial Git pack files for about nine minutes,
while a separate full clone of one affected public dependency finished in
1.1 seconds. After interrupting only the task's own build, rerunning
`swift build --disable-sandbox` with `SWIFTPM_MAX_CONCURRENT_OPERATIONS=1`
completed dependency fetching and reached compilation. Keep the task's existing
module-cache, TMPDIR and credential-helper environment settings. Preserve the
interrupted log and record the environment change in the handoff; this is an
observed workaround, not a proven diagnosis of SwiftPM internals. Do not change
package pins, clear shared caches, or interrupt another task's build to use it.
Evidence: `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/HD-MENY/ikon-impl-20260929.md` (HAVEN-root relative).

## Tracked node_modules symlinks in local test worktrees

Before `npm ci`, inspect `git ls-files -s node_modules` and `readlink node_modules`.
At CellScaffold `e5c34345` this path is a tracked symlink to `../node_modules`.
Measured 2026-10-07: npm ci replaced it with an ignored directory, emptied the
shared symlink target, and left `git status` reporting `D node_modules`; the
Node and Playwright tests passed but the clean-tree gate failed. Do not run
npm ci through such a tracked shared link. Verify the existing installation's
Playwright version against package-lock.json and use it, or install into an
isolated dependency directory without replacing the tracked link. Preserve
other worktrees' shared dependencies. Evidence: release 2026.9.6 queue job
c22 and its retained answer under `_losen-queue/done/`, and the clean c24 rerun
in `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/RELEASE/GUI-PORT-2026.9.6.md` (HAVEN-root relative).

## Cross-Repo Handoff

When behavior spans repos, include:

- code repo commit/branch
- docs repo commit/branch if changed
- app/scaffold repo commit/branch if changed
- order of application/deployment
- tests run in each repo
- known unresolved gaps

Submodule ordering:

- Always commit and push the submodule repo, for example `CellProtocol`, before
  updating the pointer in a consumer repo such as `CellScaffold` or `Binding`.
- Never include a submodule pointer update in the same commit as the submodule's
  own changes.

## Versioned package releases

For dependency migration, package publication or release planning, read
[release-versioning.md](references/release-versioning.md). The target for main
is tested semantic-version requirements with an application lockfile. Existing
revision pins remain valid during the coordinated migration; never invent a
release, retag published content or silently float a dependency to main.

Connect source work to its originating project/work item and retain exact PR,
commit, CI and release evidence. A merged PR, a published package and a verified
deployment are separate milestones. Developer Workbench coordinates the work;
Admin Workbench owns the existing operational release procedure.

## GitHub/CI

Use GitHub plugin skills when available for PR work:

- `github:yeet` for publishing a branch/PR.
- `github:gh-fix-ci` for failing CI.
- `github:gh-address-comments` for review comments.

Use `gh` CLI when connector coverage is insufficient or thread-level review
state is needed.

For a completed run, if `gh run view --log` fails because its home cache is
read-only, retrieve the authenticated log archive directly with
`gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/runs/RUN_ID/logs` and write the binary output to
an authorized evidence directory. Inspect the ZIP with Python `zipfile` or
`unzip`; preserve the run's head SHA, conclusion and exact test summaries.
This avoids changing home/cache permissions or requesting wider filesystem
access just to read CI evidence. A cache-write failure is not a CI failure.

For a single job, `gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/jobs/JOB_ID/logs` returns
the text log. If gh refuses it because it contains terminal escape sequences,
add `--allow-escape-sequences`, redirect to an authorized evidence file, and
strip ANSI/control sequences before displaying excerpts. Do not print raw
escape-bearing logs directly into the terminal or conversation.

### The `gh` binary that breaks credentials on this Mac

`/usr/local/bin/gh` is an x86_64 binary here ("Bad CPU type in executable") and
Git picks it as the credential helper, so `clone`/`fetch`/`push` fail with
`could not read Username for 'https://github.com'`. The arm64 build is
`/opt/homebrew/bin/gh`. Override it in the process environment only — never in a
Git config file:

    export GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=2
    export GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=credential.https://github.com.helper
    export GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=''
    export GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1=credential.https://github.com.helper
    export GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_1='!/opt/homebrew/bin/gh auth git-credential'

Call `/opt/homebrew/bin/gh` by its full path for every `gh` invocation.
Measured 2026-09-25: it cost two queue jobs before it was recognised.

### Workflow push rejected by HTTPS OAuth scope

Verified 2026-10-02 for the explicitly approved CellProtocol N35 CI run: the
arm64 `gh` credential helper authenticated HTTPS, but GitHub refused a push
changing `.github/workflows/security-regression-macos.yml` because that OAuth
token lacked `workflow` scope. The repository already had an SSH `origin`;
pushing the exact same branch through that existing remote succeeded. Inspect
`git remote -v` and use the configured SSH route when available and the push is
already authorized. Do not change global Git settings, broaden token scopes or
reauthenticate merely to retry this case. SSH success does not authorize a new
CI run: preserve the agreed run count, source revision and time limit.
Evidence: CellProtocol Actions run `37049974688`, head `7ef06e7`.

### The sandbox protects the `.git` of its own project root

A Codex job whose working directory is the repository cannot create
`.git/index.lock` — `git commit` fails with `Operation not permitted`, including
inside a linked worktree (`.git/worktrees/<name>/index.lock`). The same commit
succeeds when the job's working directory is the HAVEN root and the repository is
addressed by path. Measured 2026-09-25 on CellProtocolDocuments; the retry with
`cwd = HAVEN root` committed, pushed and opened the PR unchanged.

### Local verification before paid CI

Kjetil requires checking that changed code works before spending Actions minutes
(2026-09-29). Apply this before a code push, PR creation, workflow dispatch or
rerun that starts paid jobs; include automatic workflows triggered by a push.
Documentation-only changes do not require unrelated application builds.

1. Inspect the complete proposed diff and choose the affected test suites.
   Run cheap checks first: diff hygiene, manifest/pin consistency, generated
   contracts, ValueType literal limits, and the relevant script/JavaScript tests.
   Capture each command's exit status; a later successful command must not hide
   an earlier failure. If a check is a prerequisite for a mutation, enforce
   failure propagation (for example `subprocess.run(..., check=True)`) or inspect
   its nonzero exit before issuing the next tool call. Separate shell lines do
   not provide this guard. An unusable interpreter is an environment failure
   to fix, not a passing check.
2. Compile the affected application/package and test targets locally through
   the repository's approved bounded runner, then execute focused regressions.
   Swift frontend parsing does not perform type checking or linking. In PR273,
   parsing passed while a local `ideaObjects` array shadowed a method and broke
   compilation; do not use parsing as a substitute for a build. For GUI changes,
   also exercise the affected user path when a local runtime is available.
3. Record the tested source revision/diff, resolved dependency graph, platform,
   commands, exit codes and logs. Keep script tests, compiled Swift tests and
   browser evidence separate. Recheck affected evidence after code changes.
4. If capacity, cache ownership, credentials or tools block compilation, report
   the exact blocker and resolve the local environment before starting paid CI.
   Do not lower disk thresholds, invent cache ownership markers or create an
   alternate build root to bypass the blocker. A CI-only fallback needs an
   explicitly agreed bounded scope; do not silently spend minutes to discover
   basic compiler errors.
5. After local gates pass, use CI for the remaining platform/integration checks.
   Inspect current head/checks and remaining allowance first; start one run for
   the reviewed candidate and follow that run instead of issuing blind retries.

### Building while cache deletion is withheld

Observed 2026-09-30: approval review refused managed cache GC without the
cleanup plan/token. This does not authorize deletion through an ordinary build.
If capacity already passes, keep collection report-only by using an executable,
non-symlink `HAVEN_SWIFTPM_RUNNER` wrapper that invokes the existing CellProtocol
`Scripts/haven-swiftpm.sh --dry-run "$@"`. Inspect that runner first: in the
verified version `--dry-run` guards cache deletion, while Swift still executes.
Do not use `--ephemeral`, disable capacity checks, alter cache identity or raise
job limits. Keep the stable cache, lease and size/age accounting.

`run_tests_isolated.sh` invokes its fixed `run_swift_bounded.sh`, which in turn
honors `HAVEN_SWIFTPM_RUNNER`; it does not discard the override. An executable
probe that only prints a marker verified this routing without starting Swift or
GC before the real test was approved. If approval review questions the routing,
provide such direct evidence rather than bypassing the decision. The retained-
cache run then compiled and passed 100 selected Swift tests. This is a build
option, not cleanup authority; the separate plan/token and collision rules still
apply to deletion. The Xcode index remained protected by a SourceKit open-file
collision.

### Selecting the package with the bounded runner

Verified in `CellProtocol/Scripts/haven-swiftpm.sh` on 2026-10-01: the runner
derives package/cache identity from the working directory and rejects
`--package-path`, as well as caller-supplied scratch/build/cache paths. Change
into the intended package directory before invoking the approved bounded
wrapper. Do not put `--package-path` in a queued command. When reusing the
CellScaffold capacity wrapper for a sibling package, verify that its filesystem
gate covers that package and keep the shared lease, job limit and GC policy.
This source inspection corrected the HD-0156 command before execution; it does
not prove that the AgentD candidate compiled.

### Token-efficient waiting

- Treat elapsed waiting time as external-system time, not as a reason for a
  model-driven polling loop.
- Prefer one provider-native blocking watcher such as `gh run watch
  --exit-status`, one yielded command session, or a recurring monitor when the
  user requested monitoring. Do not repeatedly call `gh run view`, reload a
  browser page, or query a status endpoint through fresh model turns.
- Without a non-model monitor, make at most one initial status check and one
  terminal check per user turn. If the job remains queued or the provider has
  an incident, report that state and stop polling.
- During a GitHub Actions incident, do not rerun merely to test runner health.
  Confirm official Actions recovery first, then reuse the exact failed run or
  retry only its failed job when possible.
- Before retrying paid private-repo Actions, inspect the run's billable timing
  and remaining allowance. Keep status output limited to the fields needed for
  the decision.

## Must Not

- Do not run `git reset --hard`, `git clean`, or destructive checkout commands
  without explicit approval.
- Do not stage broad directories without inspecting what will be included.
- Do not mix unrelated CellProtocol, app, and docs work in one commit unless the
  behavior genuinely requires it.
- Do not claim CI passed without checking.

## Completion Checklist

- Status checked.
- Diff reviewed.
- Only intended files staged/committed.
- Tests/checks reported.
- Remaining dirty/untracked files disclosed when relevant.


### Coordinated local integration graph (verified 2026-10-03)

A published `DiMyMicropayments` 2026.9.3 combined with
`CELLPROTOCOL_LOCAL_PACKAGE_PATH` failed SwiftPM resolution: the stable package
selected a local/unstable CellProtocol transitively through its manifest.
Updating crypto constraints was not the remedy. The isolated CellScaffold
candidate on main `fdcfe7a5` already allowed Crypto 4.5.2, and the published
DiMy tags allowed Crypto 4.x.

For integration against an unpublished CellProtocol candidate, use isolated
source copies of the **same coordinated DiMy tags**, selecting all three via
`CELLPROTOCOL_LOCAL_PACKAGE_PATH`, `DIMYMICROPAYMENTS_LOCAL_PACKAGE_PATH` and
`DIMYMINT_LOCAL_PACKAGE_PATH`. This resolved the graph and reached App/test
compilation without editing the primary sibling checkouts. Keep exact tag SHAs
and the integration resolved graph as evidence; do not mistake it for a
publishable dependency pin or claim that compilation alone passed the tests.

The next run was blocked by the unchanged CellScaffold capacity gate after
free space decreased. A previous passing capacity check does not authorize a
later build. See the candidate's `Deliverables/Remote_use_2026-10-03/STATUS.md`
(on branch `codex/credential-remote-use-20261003`) and the handoff of the same
name in the primary CellScaffold checkout.


### Prebuilt Darwin Swift Testing and stalled CI output

Verified 2026-10-03 while investigating HavenAgentD PR28: the raw CI log ended
exactly 32768 bytes after the test-suite start, inside a passing-test line.
This is evidence to investigate output buffering, not proof that the last
printed test caused the hang. Preserve the raw artifact and distinguish the
CI toolchain from the local toolchain when reporting a reproduction.

For a Swift-Testing-only package, the selected toolchain's
`usr/libexec/swift/pm/swiftpm-testing-helper` accepts the bundle **executable**
`<bundle>.xctest/Contents/MacOS/<bundle>` with `--test-bundle-path`,
`--testing-library swift-testing` and `--no-parallel`. Set the compiled products
and SDK platform framework/library paths as required. Inherit stdout/stderr
through the watchdog instead of waiting for exit before draining pipes.
HavenAgentD's `Scripts/run_prebuilt_tests.py` checks the complete expected bundle
set and refuses future XCTest sources rather than silently omitting them.
The corrected entrypoint passed all 332 existing tests on local Xcode 27.
Do not also invoke xctest there: it already starts Swift Testing and causes a
duplicate run. Subsequent CI run 37120365881 on Xcode 26.3 passed the same 332 tests in
22.45 seconds, plus product smoke and runtime staging (head 5dc229f).

As of 2026-10-05 that entrypoint is only on `codex/file-provenance-20261002`,
not on `main`. Every run with the old `Scripts/test_haven_agentd.sh` since
2026-09-29 13:16Z has stood until the 25-minute limit at the same line: three
of three, on `main` (`ea558ba`), on that branch before the fix (`b5adebd`) and
on PR #30 (`653fb87`). The head of `main` has no green run, so a PR from `main`
is red whatever it contains.

**Before a PR is opened, and before a cause is written down for a red run, list
the runs for the base branch and for the other branches
(`gh run list --branch <base>`, then the same without `--branch`). If the base
has no green run with the same test script, the red run says nothing about the
PR.** PR #30 was opened without this, and its one run stood for 25 minutes for
a reason that had been on `main` for six days. The log was then first read as
showing that it stopped "before any of the PR's tests had started". It cannot
show that: it ends on the buffer boundary described above, and the PR's first
suite lies in the window that was never flushed. An independent check caught
the reading before it was passed on (`L-2026-10-05-les-ci-historikken-for-basisgrenen-foer-et-roedt`).

The compiled tests of a tree can be run through the runner from `5dc229f`
without checking that commit out:
`HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/PLANLAGTE-JOBBER/proev-ci-loeper.sh <commit>` as a
`skript` job takes the two Python files with `git show` into a temporary
directory. On 2026-10-05 it ran the 398 tests of PR #30's tree in 28 seconds
with Swift 6.4. That shows the tests pass through that runner on this Mac, not
that a CI run on Xcode 26.3 will be green.

Bound diagnostics and termination to the child process group created by the
runner. Darwin can report EPERM when that group contains only zombies; check
live membership before deciding termination failed. A sandbox denial executing
`ps` is a different failure and does not prove a process-group bug. Existing
binary tests do not replace compilation after Swift source or dependency changes
and do not authorize bypassing the host capacity/cache guard.


### Validate Actions contexts before spending runner minutes

GitHub rejected HavenAgentD's manual release workflow on 2026-10-04 before
creating a runner job: `runner.temp` was used in job-level `env`, where the
`runner` context is unavailable. GitHub's run annotation and actionlint 1.7.12
reported the same line. Moving that one variable into the consuming step's
`env` made both workflow files pass actionlint locally (HD-0160 / AgentD PR29).
The old manual release was not dispatched to test the fix.

For workflow-only changes, run actionlint against the affected workflows before
pushing. YAML parsing alone does not validate expression-context availability.
If a failed run has no jobs and an empty check-run list, read the run's visible
Annotations: a workflow validation error can exist without a CheckRun payload.
Use a verified official actionlint release; distinguish workflow/expression
validation from optional ShellCheck/Pyflakes checks and from execution of the
release itself. Preserve manual triggers and source pins. A context-only fix
does not require rebuilding unrelated Swift products.

Reference: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/contexts#context-availability

### Squash merge also requires workflow scope when workflows change

Observed 2026-10-05 on CellProtocol PR53: all five checks were successful and
the PR was MERGEABLE/CLEAN, but `gh pr merge --squash --match-head-commit`
was rejected by `mergePullRequest` because the OAuth token lacked `workflow`
scope for a changed workflow. The PR remained OPEN with no merge commit.
An SSH push route does not supply credentials for this GraphQL merge operation.
When instructed to stop at the first deviation, report the rejection without
changing authentication or marking the rejected merge as a completed mutation.
Evidence: `HAVEN-Deploy/_handoff/BRO/CP53-MERGE-20261005.md`.

### Source manifests and uninitialized gitlinks

For tracked mode-160000 entries, read the indexed commit with `git ls-files --stage` or the tested tree with `git ls-tree`; do not treat a directory as an initialized submodule based only on existence. `git -C <uninitialized-directory> rev-parse HEAD` can walk upward and return the parent repository HEAD. Count actual file/symlink hashes separately from gitlink commit evidence. If dependency contents are used for a gate, identify the pinned checkout/archive independently. After committing, compare tested file hashes and parent/tree pins rather than expecting the old HEAD field to match.

### Read operative queue policy separately from the status log

Before a queued host build, read the short rules section of
`_losen-queue/KOORDINERING.md` in a bounded read before its long live-thread log.
Do not concatenate long skills and the entire coordination file into a truncated
result and treat that as reading the current build policy. In s2 on 2026-10-08,
this caused an unnecessary interruption (exit 143): the worktree's older AGENTS
said ten percent, while the queue recorded Kjetil's explicit five-percent
confirmation and named the AGENTS drift as HD-0149. The current confirmation is
also sourced in `haven-disk-and-machine-health`; inspect its evidence before
changing a capacity override or concluding that cleanup is necessary.

## Fresh CellScaffold worktree: build like staging, keep the node_modules link (2026-10-08)

- `node_modules` is a **tracked symlink** to `../node_modules`. If a fresh worktree needs `npm ci` (for Playwright, say), npm replaces the link with a directory and `git status` shows ` D node_modules`. Don't commit that. Add files by name, then restore with `rm -rf node_modules && git checkout -- node_modules`.
- Without `CELLPROTOCOL_PUBLISHED_API=1`, `Package.swift` quietly uses the sibling `../CellProtocol` checkout. Build and test with `CELLPROTOCOL_PUBLISHED_API=1 swift build --build-tests` so you get the pinned version staging runs. The first build takes about 4 minutes.


## Completed Actions logs in a restricted sandbox

If gh run view --log fails while creating its default cache, set XDG_CACHE_HOME
to a writable task-specific temporary directory for that command; do not
repurpose HOME. D186 verified `XDG_CACHE_HOME=/private/tmp/d186-gh-cache gh run
view <run-id> --repo Digipomps/CellScaffold --log` against completed Linux runs.
An in-progress Actions job-log API request can return an older snapshot for
several minutes even with a new request URL. Do not infer that a test is stuck
from the stale tail. Use the completed archive for final counts and line-number
evidence; preserve failed attempts, and distinguish partial diagnostics from the
completed run. The cache location is task-local, not a repository setting.


## Immutable-candidate diagnostics when main moves

D186's permitted draft PR stopped receiving pull_request CI after main advanced
and GitHub reported mergeable=false/dirty. Do not merge unrelated main code into
an immutable-candidate investigation merely to trigger a job. When diagnostic
branches/PR operations are already authorized, an isolated base branch at the
exact candidate can preserve the source tree. The head workflow's pull_request
base-branch filter must include that diagnostic base; change the PR base and use
an ordinary PR event (close/reopen was verified) rather than a prohibited manual
dispatch. Verify the resulting PR merge tree is identical to the intended head
and verify the actual Actions checkout. Keep image/deploy jobs disabled for PR
runs and close your diagnostic PR at handoff. This is a scoped diagnostic path,
not permission to merge, tag, publish or alter unrelated PRs.
