name: haven-learning-capture
description: Use at the end of any HAVEN/CellProtocol/DiMy/Palazzo session that edited files or ran commands, or whenever a thread learns something reusable (tool, workflow, pitfall, decision, fact, skill gap): route it into the right skill/prompt/doc or a new skill with CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py and distribute it.
---

# HAVEN Learning Capture (læringsfangst)

## Purpose (formål)

F0: What one thread learns and that is useful for future development must reach
the next thread **without Kjetil asking for it** — in the skill, prompt or
documentation that thread reads, or in a new skill when none fits — on every
surface where skills are read (Claude Code, Cowork/Desktop, Codex).

Sub-purposes, and the command that serves each:

| | Purpose | Served by |
|---|---|---|
| F1 | Every thread decides at the end: reusable learning or not | `capture` / `none` / Stop-hook `hook-stop` |
| F2 | The learning lands in the *right* target, never a guess | `route`, `new-skill` |
| F3 | Updated skills reach every consumer | `sync` (Mac) or queued runner job (VM) |
| F4 | Not dependent on memory | Stop-hook, entrypoint block in `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`, `check --strict` |
| F5 | Traceable: what, when, who, where it went, whether it happened | `Documentation/Learnings/ledger.jsonl` + `LEDGER.md` |
| F6 | Claims are verifiable | `applied` (hash check), `check --drift`, `hash`, `validate` |

The failure this removes: Kjetil has had to explain the same thing to thread
after thread because learnings stayed in a reply or a log nobody re-reads.

## Tool

`CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py` — pure stdlib, runs on the Mac and in the
Cowork VM against the mount (connected folder `~/Build/Digipomps/HAVEN`, run from
the HAVEN root). The HAVEN root is a device setting (`HAVEN_ROOT`, `~/.haven/root`, else derived from the script's
location); never hardcode it in skills, docs or code. On the Mac the wrapper
`~/.haven/bin/haven-learn` is installed by `install --home`.

Full documentation: `CellScaffold/Documentation/Learnings/README.md`.

## What counts as a learning (and what does not)

Capture when future threads would otherwise re-discover or repeat it:

- **tool** — a command, script, flag or route that worked (or an API/MCP fact verified this session)
- **workflow** — a sequence that avoided a known failure
- **pitfall** — something that documentably did not work, with the cause if known (`ukjent` otherwise)
- **decision** — a choice Kjetil made that constrains future work (quote his words)
- **fact** — an environment fact (path, version, which store a client reads) verified by reading the source
- **gap** — a skill/prompt/doc that was wrong or missing when you needed it

Do not capture: session narrative (log), deploy-specific lessons (`hd lesson new`),
PDD lessons that already live in `Book/haven_lessons_register_v0.json` (capture
only the *routing* if a skill must change), or planned behaviour.

## Procedure

1. **Route before you write.** `python3 CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py route --query "<keywords>"`
   lists candidate skills, prompts and docs with paths. Choose the target the
   *next* thread will read. No fit → `--target new-skill:<kebab-name> --description "<when to use>"`.
2. **Capture.** `python3 CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py capture --what "<one sentence>" --kind <tool|workflow|pitfall|decision|fact|gap> --target skill:<name> [--why …] [--source …] [--evidence …] [--tags a,b]`
   The entry records the target's hash so "applied" can be verified. One line is
   also appended under today's heading in `Losen/logg/Losen_Hendelseslogg.md`.
   Edited the target before capturing? Add `--applied`.
3. **Update the target yourself, in the same session.** Edit the SKILL.md /
   prompt / doc with the learning where the next reader needs it (a "Known
   pitfalls" or "Read first" line, a corrected path, a new step). Keep it
   current-state only; never document planned behaviour as implemented.
   From a Cowork thread, write into `.claude/` via the device shell (`cp`), not via file commit.
4. **Verify.** `python3 CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py applied <id>` — refuses
   if the target is byte-identical to capture time.
5. **Distribute.** `python3 CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py sync --id <id>`
   - On the Mac: runs `CellScaffold/scripts/sync_claude_desktop_skills.py --skill <name> --include-user --include-codex --build-artifacts` → `~/.claude/skills`, `~/.codex/skills`, the Desktop local-agent store, and the upload ZIP under `CellScaffold/Documentation/ClaudeSkills/desktop-zips/`.
   - In the Cowork VM: writes a job to `.agent-spool/inbox/` for `.agent-tools/agent_job_runner.py` (allowlisted). Say so to Kjetil; the entry becomes `synced` when the job's `mark-synced` step runs.
   - Queue one sync at a time from the VM. The job file is named by the second
     (`haven-learn-sync-<date>-<time>.json`), so two `sync --id` calls in the same second
     write the same file and only the last id is marked `synced`, with no error
     (measured 2026-10-03). Wait until the job has left `.agent-spool/inbox/`, then run
     `check` and confirm the id is gone from the list before queuing the next.
   - Prompts and docs travel with git, not sync. Cowork plugin copies are uploaded ZIPs — they do not update themselves; say when a ZIP needs re-upload.
   - Measured 2026-09-29: the Desktop store bucket `skills-plugin/b766ceb2…/1d27db43…` is re-synced from the
     account by the app within minutes, so a local sync there does not stick. For Cowork/Desktop the durable
     route is the uploaded ZIP (`desktop-zips/<name>.zip`) or a skill card; skills outside the CellScaffold canon get
     their ZIP from `build-zip --skill <name>`. `check --drift --verbose` names the copy and its mtime.
6. **Nothing reusable?** `python3 CellScaffold/scripts/haven_learn.py none --reason "<short, honest>"` — the decision is recorded (unversioned `.agent-spool/haven-learn/none.jsonl`), so the Stop-hook releases the session.

## Stop-hook (Claude Code on the Mac)

`install --home` registers `"$HOME/.haven/bin/haven-learn" hook-stop` as a Stop
hook in `~/.claude/settings.json` (user scope → every project). When a session
edited files or ran commands and has not run `capture`/`none`, the hook blocks
the stop once with the exact commands (including `--session <id>`), then lets
the next stop through (`stop_hook_active`). `HAVEN_LEARN_HOOK_MODE=always|edits|off`.
Cowork threads have no hook: the entrypoint block and Kjetil's saved preference
carry the same rule; Codex reads it from `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` and `HAVEN/AGENTS.md`.

## Session-end checklist

- [ ] `route` was run before choosing a target (or `new-skill` created)
- [ ] `capture` recorded the learning with kind, target, source
- [ ] the target file was actually edited (`applied <id>` passed)
- [ ] `sync` ran on the Mac, or the queued job path was reported to Kjetil
- [ ] `check --strict` from the HAVEN root exits 0 (no open entries)
- [ ] relative paths in what you wrote; absolute paths only in the chat to Kjetil

## Boundaries

- Never mark `applied` with `--force` without `--note` explaining why the hash did not change.
- Never edit Book 23 (purpose tree) canonically through this skill — use the PDD capture process.
- Never write secrets, IPs beyond the host IP, or e-mail addresses into the ledger.
- A learning captured but not applied is an open item; `check --strict` stays red until it is applied or `drop --reason`.

